tion against state Senator David Brock Smith, who secured the Republican nomination in May's primary. Oregon leans Democratic by eight points, and no general-election poll has been published, so the assessment rests on fundamentals, expert ratings, and prediction markets. Every major rater — Cook, Inside Elections, Sabato's Crystal Ball, the Silver Bulletin, and others — places the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column.\n\nMoney underscores that picture: Merkley has raised about $4.5 million and holds $6.8 million on hand, while Smith has raised roughly $121,000 with under $30,000 available. Markets on Kalshi and Polymarket price Merkley near 98 percent, with PredictIt at 93. With a low-profile, lightly funded challenger and no polling suggesting competition, this ranks among the least contested Senate races on the 2026 map.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9939, "margin": 17.74}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9912, "margin": 17.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 17.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9889, "margin": 17.5}]}